### Layer 3: Linear Algebra, Kronecker Product, and Graph Theory (≈ 640 bits)

**Matrices.** A matrix A of dimensions m × n over a field 𝔽 is an array of m × n elements of 𝔽, organized in m rows and n columns. Matrix addition and scalar multiplication are defined elementwise. Matrix multiplication of an m × n matrix A by an n × p matrix B produces the m × p matrix C where C_ij = Σ_k A_ik B_kj.

**The Kronecker Product — Explicit Bootstrap.** For an m × n matrix A and a p × q matrix B, the Kronecker product A ⊗ B is the mp × nq matrix defined as follows:

```
Replace each scalar entry a_ij of A with the p×q block  a_ij · B.
Arrange these blocks in the same row-column pattern as A.
Read the resulting block matrix row-major to obtain A ⊗ B.
```

Concretely: the block in rows (i−1)p+1 through ip and columns (j−1)q+1 through jq of A ⊗ B is equal to a_ij · B. This construction uses only scalar multiplication and block concatenation, both defined from matrix arithmetic above. For A ∈ 𝔽^{m×n} and B ∈ 𝔽^{p×q}:

```
(A ⊗ B)_{(i−1)p+r, (j−1)q+s} = A_{ij} × B_{rs}
for i = 1,…,m; j = 1,…,n; r = 1,…,p; s = 1,…,q.
```

**Stochastic Matrices.** A square matrix T of dimension d × d over ℝ is stochastic if all entries are non-negative and all row sums equal 1. T represents the transition probabilities of a finite-state dynamical system: T_ij is the probability that the system transitions from state i to state j in one step.

**Stationary Distribution.** For an ergodic stochastic matrix T, the unique stationary distribution π is the probability vector satisfying π T = π, with all π_i > 0 and Σ_i π_i = 1. This vector is the left eigenvector of T corresponding to eigenvalue 1, computable by standard linear algebra (power iteration or Gaussian elimination over ℝ), both of which are definable from Layer 2 arithmetic.

**Directed Graphs.** A directed graph G = (V, E) is a pair of a finite vertex set V and an edge multiset E ⊆ V × V. The adjacency matrix, the transition matrix (adjacency matrix normalized by row sums), and the concept of a bipartition B = (B₁, B₂) of V (a partition of V into two disjoint non-empty subsets) are defined.

